Title: "Precedence"
Id: "affb5e64-17a9-4b53-8119-1b6feae01023"

Text: "Expression forms bind from loosest to tightest as follows:

1. function literal `=>` (right-associative, loosest)
2. specification and predicate forms: `is`, `is?`, `is_not?`, quoted `\"op\"`
   specs and predicates, infix specs `\\:...:/` and `\\:...?:/`, `member_of`,
   `satisfies`, and spec literals
3. infix command expressions `\\.cmd./`
4. equality `=` and `!=`, and special binary operators such as `<` and `>`
5. additive `+`, `-`
6. multiplicative `*`, `/`
7. power `^`
8. named operators `|op|`, including member paths `|M.*|` and the colon variants
9. unary prefix (`+`, `-`, and prefix named operators `f|`)
10. postfix named operators (`x |f`)
11. atoms (tightest)

Note that infix commands (level 3) bind **looser** than arithmetic, while named
operators (level 8) bind **tighter**; they are not the same level.

Associativity: `^` and `=>` associate to the right; `=`, the arithmetic levels,
named operators, and infix commands all associate to the left, so `a |f| b |g| c`
is a left-associated chain.

Because equality binds tighter than infix command expressions:

```mlg-fragment
x = a \\.or./ x = b
```

is parsed like:

```mlg-fragment
(x = a) \\.or./ (x = b)
```

Use dot-grouped source parentheses `(. ... .)` to make grouping explicit without
forcing visible rendered parentheses."
Id: "4ebc95ac-e6aa-4fbb-93b1-6226cf039467"
